---
title: "Style utilities"
description: "Values in style, one custom property per decision."
section: Utilities
sectionOrder: 3
order: 0
---

A style utility is a custom property in an element's `style` attribute: `style="--p: 4; --w--md: fit-content"`. The rule is Tailwind's names, CSS values: a utility is named after the Tailwind class it stands in for (`--justify`, `--items`; `border-radius` is `--radius`), and it takes the value its CSS property takes (`--justify: space-between`, `--w: 100%`). If you know the property and not the class, look it up in the [property-to-utility table](/docs/concepts/utilities-reference/).

The kit matches the attribute text, so spelling is exact: `--<utility>: <value>`, one space after the colon, declarations separated by `; `.

{% preview src="utilities/compose" /%}

## What a value can be

- **A scale number** on spacing, margin, sizing, and radius utilities: `--px: 6` is `6 × --spacing`, and `--radius: 2.5` is the same step times `--radius-multiplier` (`--radius-md`). The space tokens sit on this scale (2xs 1, xs 2, sm 4, md 6, lg 11, xl 24, 2xl 40), so write the number and skip the `var()`.
- **A length or any CSS value**: `--w: 100%`, `--radius: 1rem`, `--shadow: 0 1px 2px black`.
- **A token by name**, on a few utilities, at every tier: `--font-weight: strong`, `--radius: full`, `--display: flex-col`. Other tokens take the `var()` form: `--shadow: var(--shadow-md)`.
- **A keyword** where the property takes one: `--position: sticky`, `--w: fit-content`, `--grid-cols: subgrid`.

## One space after the colon

A tier gate reads the property itself, so `--p:4` and `--w--md:6` work with any spacing. A keyword (`auto`, `subgrid`), a token by name (`strong`, `full`), a `--display` shorthand, `--bg: none`, and `--position: sticky` are matched as text, and the text the kit looks for has one space after the colon. Written without it, `--w:auto` is 0 wide and `--radius:full` stays square. The audit (`<ui-debug>`, the editor, and the ESLint plugin) reports both, and the formatter writes the space back.

The place this bites is a framework's object `style`: React and Vue serialize `{ "--w": "auto" }` as `--w:auto`, and JSX is outside the linter. There, write those values as a string `style`, or in the `var()` form (`"--shadow": "var(--shadow-md)"`).

## Rules that bite

1. A tier needs a base unless the utility works tier-only (the tables say which): write `--text: currentColor; --text--hover: red`, not `--text--hover: red` alone. Padding and `--bg` work tier-only on a plain element (`--px--md: 8`, `--bg--hover: var(--color-muted)`), since the reset leaves it unpadded and unpainted; where a primitive pads or paints the element itself, its value wins unless it reads the utility. `--text`, margin, and `--font-size` do not, because the base sets a link's color, the margins between text blocks, and a heading's size, and a tier-only value would reset them at rest.
2. Breakpoints reach layout and color; states reach color and effects. `--px--hover` does not exist. The anchor utilities (`--anchor-name`, `--position-anchor`, `--position-area`) take both.
3. A utility on a primitive flattens its states: `--bg: red` on a button is red on hover too. Add the state tier, or set the hook (`--ui-button-bg`) instead.
4. Breakpoints read the nearest inline-size container, not the window. See [breakpoints](/api/foundations/breakpoints/).

Every utility that writes a property is registered with `@property` as non-inheriting, so a value never leaks into a child; the `--before-*` / `--after-*` forms are the exception, read on the pseudo-element and never registered. Utilities live in `@layer ui.utilities` at zero specificity: they beat component rules by layer order, and any rule you write in `overrides` beats them.
